// The V4A patch a constrained `apply_patch` call is decoded under (D172 §9), beside its
// parser, `apply_patch.py`. Written for this project from that parser, not copied: the parser
// takes everything this grammar admits (`test_apply_patch_grammar.py` holds the two together),
// and is more lenient only where a reply needs it and a constrained call cannot (text around the
// envelope, a blank line, no space after a header's colon).
//
// What the parser refuses, so does this: an `*** Add File` holds only `+` lines, a `*** Delete
// File` none, a `*** Move to` only directly after its `*** Update File` line (D196 §10.3, which
// amends D172 §10), and every hunk of an `*** Update File` changes a line, which a move may
// leave with no hunk at all.
// Two refusals stay the parser's alone, a grammar having no memory: one section per file, and a
// hunk whose `-` and `+` lines say the same thing.
//
// Kept to the Lark that OpenAI's decoder (LLGuidance) reads: no lookarounds, no priorities, no
// imports.

start: begin section+ end

begin: "*** Begin Patch" LF
end: "*** End Patch" LF?

section: add_file | update_file | delete_file

add_file: "*** Add File: " PATH LF added*
added: "+" TEXT? LF

update_file: "*** Update File: " PATH LF (move_to body? | body)
move_to: "*** Move to: " PATH LF
body: scope* hunk (scope+ hunk?)* end_of_file?
delete_file: "*** Delete File: " PATH LF
scope: "@@" TEXT? LF
hunk: kept* changed (kept | changed)*
kept: " " TEXT? LF
changed: ("-" | "+") TEXT? LF
end_of_file: "*** End of File" LF

PATH: /[^\s][^\n]*/
TEXT: /[^\n]+/
LF: "\n"
